'''Sakai Prefecture''' ({{lang|ja|堺県}}, ''Sakai-ken'') is an island [[Prefectures of Okaiken|prefecture]] of [[Okaiken]] located in the [[Nankai]] Region in the [[Sea of Mitoyagi]], approximately 120 km west of the Yagikoshu mainland. With a population of 817,393 as of 2026, it is Okaiken's premier resort island—a dense, subtropical destination of white-sand beaches, volcanic hot springs, and theme parks, centred on the active volcano of [[Mount Sakai]] ({{nihongo|堺岳|Sakai-dake}}, 842 m), which dominates the island's centre with frequent Strombolian eruptions.

Sakai Prefecture is the {{nihongo|"Playground of Yagikoshu"|焼木子の遊庭|Yagikoshu no Asaniwa}}—a place where the volcanic energy that powers the onsen also powers the roller coasters, and where the island's compact geography packs beaches, mountains, and entertainment into a space barely 40 km across.

== History ==

=== The resort island ===
[[File:Sakai Theme Park.jpg|thumb|left|250px|The Sakai Theme Park, the island's original resort attraction]]
Sakai's transformation from a remote volcanic island to Okaiken's premier resort destination began in the 1960s, when the [[Saito Restoration]] government's economic planners identified the island's unique combination of volcanic hot springs, subtropical climate, and proximity to the Yagikoshu mainland as the foundation for a tourism industry. The {{nihongo|Sakai Resort Development Act|堺リゾート開発法|Sakai Rizōto Kaihatsu-hō}} of 1967 designated the island as a {{nihongo|Special Tourism Zone|特別観光区|Tokubetsu Kankō-ku}}, providing tax incentives and infrastructure investment that attracted the first wave of resort developers.

The opening of the {{nihongo|Sakai Theme Park|堺テーマパーク|Sakai Tēma Pāku}} in 1972—marketed as the {{nihongo|"Volcano Park"|火山水上楽園|Kazan Suijō Rakuen}}—established the island's identity as a family entertainment destination. The park's signature attraction, the {{nihongo|"Eruption Coaster"|噴火コースター|Funka Kōsutā}}, simulated a Strombolian eruption using the island's actual geothermal steam vents, creating a ride experience that was, in the words of its designer, "half engineering, half geology."

=== The Strombolian island ===
[[File:Mount Sakai Strombolian Eruption.jpg|thumb|right|250px|A Strombolian eruption of Mount Sakai, visible from the island's beaches]]
Mount Sakai is one of the most active volcanoes in Okaiken, producing small, spectacular Strombolian eruptions—explosive bursts of incandescent lava fragments and ash—every 2–6 weeks. The eruptions are typically small enough to be viewed safely from designated observation points, and the volcano has become the island's most popular tourist attraction, with the {{nihongo|Sakai Volcano Observatory|堺火山観測所|Sakai Kazan Kansokusho}} operating a live webcam and eruption prediction service.

The frequent eruptions have shaped the island's culture and economy. The {{nihongo|"Fire Festival"|火祭り|Hi Matsuri}}—held whenever the volcano produces a particularly spectacular eruption—draws thousands of visitors who gather on the beaches to watch the lava fountains against the night sky. The volcanic ash also fertilises the island's soil, supporting a distinctive tropical agriculture.

== Geography ==

Sakai Prefecture is an island prefecture, comprising the main island of Sakai and several smaller satellite islands in the Sea of Mitoyagi. The main island is roughly circular, approximately 40 km in diameter, with the volcanic peak of Mount Sakai at its centre. The island's coastline is a succession of white-sand beaches and rocky headlands, with the most developed resort areas concentrated on the eastern and southern coasts, which are sheltered from the prevailing winds.

The satellite islands—{{nihongo|Oshima-Sakai|大島堺}}, {{nihongo|Niijima|新島}}, {{nihongo|Toshima-Sakai|利島堺}}, and {{nihongo|Kamishima|神島}}—are smaller volcanic islands to the east and south, with small fishing communities and limited tourism infrastructure.

=== Climate ===
Sakai Prefecture has a [[w:Tropical monsoon climate|tropical monsoon climate]] (''Am''), with warm, humid conditions year-round. Summer temperatures consistently exceed 30°C, and winter temperatures rarely drop below 18°C. The island receives significant rainfall during the November–December rainy season and the November–April typhoon season, but the winter months are typically dry and sunny, making Sakai a popular winter escape from the cooler Yagikoshu mainland.

