== Economy ==

Sakai Prefecture's economy is dominated by tourism, with the resort and entertainment industry accounting for the majority of employment and revenue. The prefecture's GDP was approximately 円32 billion in 2025, with services (tourism, hospitality, entertainment) accounting for 68%, industry 18%, and agriculture/fisheries 14%.

=== Tourism and theme parks ===
[[File:Sakai Onsen Village.jpg|thumb|right|250px|The Sakai Onsen Village at the base of Mount Sakai]]
Sakai is Okaiken's most visited island destination, attracting approximately 12 million visitors annually. The tourism industry is centred on three sectors: the {{nihongo|onsen|温泉}} resort industry, the theme park and entertainment industry, and the beach and marine leisure industry.

The island's five major theme parks—including the original {{nihongo|Sakai Theme Park|堺テーマパーク}}, the {{nihongo|Sakai Water Kingdom|堺水上王国|Sakai Suijō Ōkoku}}, and the {{nihongo|Volcano Adventure Park|火山アドベンチャーパーク|Kazan Adobenchā Pāku}}—collectively attract approximately 8 million visitors annually and employ approximately 12,000 workers. The parks are known for their innovative use of the island's volcanic energy: geothermal steam powers the rides, volcanic hot water heats the water park pools, and the frequent eruptions of Mount Sakai provide a natural "special effects" backdrop that no mainland park can match.

The {{nihongo|Sakai Onsen|堺温泉}} belt, stretching along the southern coast at the base of Mount Sakai, comprises approximately 40 hot spring resorts and ryokan inns. The onsen are fed directly by the volcano's geothermal system, producing some of the hottest and most mineral-rich spring water in Okaiken. The {{nihongo|Sakai Onsen Village|堺温泉郷|Sakai Onsen-gō}} is a designated [[National Important Cultural Landscape]].

=== Tropical agriculture ===
The volcanic soils and tropical climate support the cultivation of tropical fruits that cannot be grown on the Yagikoshu mainland, including mangoes, pineapples, bananas, and sugarcane. The {{nihongo|Sakai Mango|堺マンゴー|Sakai Mangō}} is a GI-protected product, and the island's sugar cane supports a small but historically significant {{nihongo|brown sugar|黒糖|kokutō}} industry. The {{nihongo|Sakai Kokutō|堺黒糖|Sakai Kokutō}} is a premium artisanal product, produced using traditional methods in small batches.

=== Volcanic glass and obsidian ===
The frequent eruptions of Mount Sakai produce large quantities of volcanic glass and obsidian, which have been harvested by the island's artisans for centuries. The {{nihongo|Sakai Obsidian Workshop|堺黒曜石工房|Sakai Kokuyōseki Kōbō}} produces decorative objects, jewellery, and architectural elements from the volcanic glass, and the material has become a signature souvenir of the island. The obsidian industry employs approximately 800 artisans and generates approximately 円2.8 billion in annual revenue.
Sakai maintains a world-renowned reputation in precision cutlery forging and historical maritime logistics. Artisan blacksmiths utilize traditional Kazan-tetsu pyrometallurgical methods, hand-hammering high-carbon steel blades that are differentially quenched to achieve razor-sharp edges and enduring toughness. These knives are sought after by master chefs worldwide, while the prefecture's modern commercial harbor handles coastal freight distribution and marine bunkering for vessels traversing the Seto Inland Sea.

== Transportation ==

=== Air ===
[[Sakai Airport]] (IATA: SKI) operates frequent flights to [[Okami]], [[Toyozawa]], and [[Shinagawa]], with additional seasonal services. The airport is the primary gateway for the majority of visitors.

=== Sea ===
The {{nihongo|Sakai Ferry|堺フェリー|Sakai Ferī}} operates high-speed catamaran services from [[Kii]] (90 minutes) and [[Mutsu]] (120 minutes), with regular car ferry services for visitors bringing vehicles.

== Culture ==

=== Cuisine ===
Sakai's cuisine is tropical and volcanic. The island's signature dish is {{nihongo|''Sakai kokutō zenzai''|堺黒糖ぜんざい}}—a sweet red bean soup made with Sakai brown sugar, served cold as a tropical dessert. Other specialities include {{nihongo|''Sakai mango parfait''|堺マンゴーパフェ}}, fresh tropical fruit platters, and {{nihongo|''volcano curry''|火山カレー|Kazan Karē}}—a spicy curry served in a heated stone bowl that sizzles like the volcano, a dish created for the theme parks.

=== Festivals ===
* {{nihongo|'''Fire Festival'''|火祭り|Hi Matsuri}} — Spontaneous festival held during major eruptions, drawing thousands of beach-side spectators
* {{nihongo|'''Sakai Summer Festival'''|堺夏祭り|Sakai Natsu Matsuri}} — Annual beach and fireworks festival (July), the largest in the Sea of Mitoyagi
* {{nihongo|'''Kokutō Festival'''|黒糖祭り|Kokutō Matsuri}} — Brown sugar harvest festival (December)

